  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nNear Record Warmth & Dry Conditions Through Tuesday...\nMid-level warm air advection will continue to strengthen a \nprominent upper-level ridge centered over the mid-Mississippi \nValley, yielding near-record warmth across the region over the \nnext two days. Afternoon high temperatures are forecast to top \nout around 90 degrees on Monday across central and southeast \nIllinois, with similar warmth persisting Tuesday for areas east \nof the Illinois River. A strong 850\u2013800 mb warm nose, with \ntemperatures approaching 20C, will maintain a robust capping \ninversion across central Illinois, ensuring dry weather through \nearly Tuesday evening.\n\nActive Pattern & Precipitation Chances Wednesday and Thursday...\nA series of mid-level shortwaves tracking across southern \nCanada will gradually push the low-to-mid level baroclinic zone\nsouth toward the Midwest late Tuesday into Wednesday. A modest \nshortwave propagating along this boundary will enhance a low- \nlevel jet to our west Tuesday night, initiating convective \nactivity that will spread into west-central Illinois overnight. \nModest instability (MLCAPE around 500 J/kg) paired with strong \ndeep-layer shear (around 40 kt) will support a low-end severe \nthreat late Tuesday night, primarily across portions of the\nlower Illinois River Valley, before instability wanes further \neast.\n\nThe frontal boundary is projected to stall across central \nIllinois Wednesday into Thursday, bringing recurring \nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms. Temperatures will \ndisplay a sharp gradient across the boundary, ranging from the \nlow-to-mid 70s north to the 80s south. LREF total QPF from \nTuesday night through Friday morning is expected to average 0.90\nto 1.25 inches across central Illinois, with a 20-30% \nprobability of exceeding 1.50 inches between the I-74 and I-70 \ncorridors (highest probabilities west of Springfield). NBM \nnumbers, meanwhile, paint slightly higher totals of 1.30 to 2.00\ninches through Friday morning. Given the recent dry spell, the\noverall concern for flooding is low.\n\nCooler Pattern Change Friday into the Weekend...\nBy Friday, the boundary will sag south of the area, leaving \nonly lingering morning showers before strong surface high \npressure builds southeast from the Northern Plains. This \nshifting pattern will introduce a significantly cooler air mass,\nwith high temperatures dropping into the 60s on Friday and \nupper 50s to lower 60s for Saturday. Light winds and clearing \nskies beneath the surface ridge axis will allow overnight lows \nto fall into the 30s Friday and Saturday nights, bringing the \npotential for the first widespread frost of the season.",
